extraction_notes: "WB19 Australia printed 56-59 (cache p057-p060, cache page = printed folio + 1), read off the text layer in column order. Heading Bushman O.C.C. on printed 56, the three types on printed 57-58, Special Abilities and the stat block on printed 58, the rest of the stat block on printed 59. || GROUP: listed under Outback O.C.C.s, Australian Outback Occupational Character Classes, on printed 56; the entry states no S.D.C. base, only +2D6+20 to physical S.D.C., so men_of_arms is false and occ_group is optional. || XP: the Raider, Technologist, Guide/Scout/Hunter & Bushman ladder on the Experience Tables, printed 224, read off a render. || THREE TYPES: Explorer, Desert Bushman or Nomad, and the classic Bushman are a pick-one special_abilities group; they share one stat block. Their differences are in the options text and are NOT applied: the Nomad +10% Internal Compass, the Explorer +20% on the second Memory for Details number, the Nomad-only Assassin, and the Classic psionics. || PSIONICS: minor, I.S.P. equal to M.E. plus 1D6 per level, the four named powers granted outright. The Classic Bushman bonus of 2D6+6 I.S.P. at levels 2, 6 and 12 (the sentence reads as replacing the 1D6 per level for that type) and his third-level Sixth Sense plus one Sensitive or Physical power of choice are not stored, because they belong to one of three types; they are in the type option and GM Notes. Empathy and Hypnotic Suggestion work on animals only; that limit is prose. || BONUSES: +2D6+20 S.D.C., +1D6 P.S., +1D4 P.E., +1D4 M.E., +3 pull punch, +2 initiative stored. +1 to save vs mind control and illusions is mind_control 1 and illusionary_magic 1 (a reading of illusions). +3 vs disease, poison and pain is disease 3, toxins_poisons 3, pain 3. +1 vs Horror Factor at levels 1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12 and 15 is horror_factor 1 flat plus seven at_level entries. || SPECIAL ABILITIES: Internal Compass 60% +3% and Memory for Details 60%/30% +3% have no catalog skill row and are special_abilities prose. || SKILL READINGS: Speaks Australian/English is Language: Australian English at 90 with the catalog per-level; Tracking is Tracking (people); Identify Plants is Identify Plants & Fruit; Skin and Prepare Animal Hides is Skin & Prepare Animal Hides; General Athletics is Athletics (general); W.P. Automatic Rifle is W.P. Automatic and Semi-automatic Rifles. Hand to Hand: Expert one skill, Martial Arts three, Assassin two for the Nomad only (stored with a condition, shown not enforced). || RELATED: two Wilderness or Espionage plus three other is count 5 with a union floor of 2. Military prints Camouflage +15% and Trap Construction +6%; one bonus per category is held, so 6 is stored and Camouflage is 9% short, said in the note. Electrical Basic only is Basic Electronics; Mechanical Automotive only is Automotive Mechanics. Pilot any except military vehicles, Power Armor, Bots and Ships excludes the robot, power armor and military rows plus Boat: Ships and Space: Starship. The book files Horsemanship under Pilot (printed 151-152); the catalog has its own Horsemanship category, offered as General and Exotic Animals, while Horsemanship: Cowboy sits in the Cowboy category the book closes. || MONEY: 1D6 x $100 in cash; Australian dollars equal credits. || EQUIPMENT not stored as rows: full brim hat, spoon, ball of twine, six small metal pikes or wooden stakes, roll of aluminum foil, large roll of plastic, the optional conventional binoculars or telescope, compass and a laser (the book says may also have, and does not say what kind of laser), the S.D.C. rifle with 2D6x10 bullets or 1D4+3 extra clips (the text layer prints 2D6xlO), one M.D. weapon of choice and one other weapon reflective of a W.P., and the Nomad robes, hood, face mask and lighter shoes. They are in GM Notes. Boomerang type is not printed; the average boomerang row is used. Silt goggles are the Nomad only and are stored with that note."
---

## Lore

A Bushman is an Outbacker who has gone bush: not an Aborigine, but someone
who has taken the wild as home almost as completely as one. Where a Jackaroo
is sociable and easygoing, the Bushman is gritty, serious and hardened by what
he has seen out there. Some drift back to towns now and then to see friends
and family; many are happiest alone, living by their wits.

They are fierce fighters, good go-betweens with the Aboriginal tribes and the
best early warning a party or an Outback settlement can have. In a group they
are reliable, but tend to range ahead, behind or on the flanks rather than
walk in the middle. Cities bewilder them, modern conveniences are often
unfamiliar, and people who lean on machines have to earn their respect.

There are three kinds. The Explorer lives to see what nobody has seen and
trades what he learns. The Desert Nomad has made the open wastes his home and
is the one person likely to get you across them alive. The classic Bushman,
the most common, keeps some humor and a liking for company, with little
patience for cruelty, waste or nonsense.

## GM Notes

Choose the type at creation. What the sheet does not apply for it: the Desert
Nomad''s Internal Compass is 70% rather than 60%; the Explorer''s second Memory
for Details number is 50% rather than 30%; only the Nomad may take Hand to
Hand: Assassin (two skill selections); and the Classic Bushman gains 2D6+6
I.S.P. at levels 2, 6 and 12 rather than 1D6 per level, plus Sixth Sense and
one Sensitive or Physical power of choice at level 3.

Percentile abilities the sheet does not roll: Internal Compass 60% +3% per
level; Memory for Details 60%/30% +3% per level.

Empathy and Hypnotic Suggestion work only on natural, normal animals.

Skills are relative to terrain: a Desert Nomad making a survival roll in the
bush, or another Bushman in the desert, is at -25% at the G.M.''s discretion,
and the Nomad''s Blend applies to the desert.

Camouflage as a related skill is +15%, not the +6% the sheet adds.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: a full brim hat, spoon, ball of
twine, six small metal pikes or wooden stakes, a roll of aluminum foil, a
large roll of plastic and other survival pieces; possibly conventional
binoculars or a telescope, a compass and a laser. Weapons: an S.D.C. rifle
(likely with a flash suppressor, no telescopic sight) with 2D6x10 bullets or
1D4+3 extra clips, one M.D. weapon of choice and one other weapon reflecting a
W.P. Nomads wear sand-colored hooded cotton robes with a face mask and lighter
shoes in place of fatigues and boots.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'bushman');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'bushman';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-bushman-class.sql');
